  Cell["Reconstruct every later slice", "Section"], 
  Cell[TextData[{StyleBox["CausalSpatialGraphs", "InlineCode"], " joins \
events with a common ancestor in the preceding slice. The first slice has no \
past in this finite history, so its spatial graph has no edges. Each later \
graph has 27 vertices and 90 edges, with displacements ", 
     Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"\[PlusMinus]", SubscriptBox["e", "i"]}], 
        TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " and ", 
     Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"\[PlusMinus]", "(", SubscriptBox["e", 
           "i"], "-", SubscriptBox["e", "j"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], 
      "InlineFormula"], "."}], "Text"], 
  Cell[TextData[
    {"Intersect each chain with the actual slice to obtain its frame. ", 
     StyleBox["FindInfraCoordinateAxes", "InlineCode"], 
     " selects oriented geodesic axes, and ", 
     StyleBox["RectilinearCoordinates", "InlineCode"], 
     " projects onto them. Here the three axes are ", 
     Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"(", "-", SubscriptBox["e", "i"], ",", "0", 
          ",", SubscriptBox["e", "i"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], 
      "InlineFormula"], ". In general, the automatic axes extend shortest \
germs without increasing the excess of interval vertices over path vertices. \
This is a specified selection rule; explicit axes can instead be supplied \
with ", StyleBox["\"Axes\"", "InlineCode"], 
     ". Nearby spatial direction points can be selected from an ", 
     StyleBox["InfraShell", "InlineCode"], 
     "; their causal continuation remains additional frame data."}], "Text"], 
  Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"spatialGraphs", " ", "=", " ", 
